cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this formalization, please cite it as below."
title: "Magic square of squares: known facts, checked in Lean"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Carlson
    given-names: Drew
repository-code: "https://github.com/drew2apps/magic-square-of-squares-lean"
license: Apache-2.0
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.24435351
    description: "Zenodo concept DOI; resolves to the latest archived version"
version: 0.5.0
date-released: 2026-11-23
abstract: >-
  Lean 4 proofs of the known necessary conditions and reformulations for a 3x3 magic square of
  squares (an open problem), and kernel-checked conditions on two ratios of sums of edge roots.
  For the ratio m : n of b + d to h + f: it avoids 484 residue classes at 34 primes, and
  m^2 + n^2 is a square modulo 7, 17, 31 and 47 and divides 8 e^2, where e is the centre root.
  With the second ratio m' : n' of b + d to f - h: 2 (m^2 + n^2)(m'^2 + n'^2) is a square, and
  either a prime congruent to 1 modulo 8 divides e and divides both numbers to an odd power, or
  one of them is a square and the other is twice a square. One edge root exceeds 500 (far below
  published search bounds). Computed and not formalized: a census of the ratios with m <= 1000
  (of 425 ratios left by congruences, 268 are closed by rank 0 and 122 by rank 1; 9 more by an
  argument with four maps that has not been reviewed; 2 more, counted apart, by a two-cover descent
  run in Magma; 24 are open). The repository also contains experiments that are not counted in
  the census and that nobody has reviewed: two descents over the Gaussian integers and a proposed
  proof for the last pair of ratios left by them.
  The problem itself is not solved, and no novelty is claimed.
  The Lean code and the text were written with Claude, an AI model made by Anthropic.
keywords:
  - Lean 4
  - Mathlib
  - magic square of squares
  - formal verification
